{#- One <picture> macro for the whole site.

    Each photo was six lines of near-identical markup with two long srcset
    strings in it, twenty-eight times over. The widths, the fallback file and the
    intrinsic dimensions all come from _data/photos.js, which reads them off the
    files in assets/ — so the only things a page has to say are which photo, what
    it shows, and how wide it will be drawn.

      name    the stem, e.g. work-cargo, with no width or extension
      alt     what the photograph shows. Never decorative on this site.
      sizes   measured off the rendered page, not guessed. Phone value first.
      eager   true for anything above the fold, which drops loading=lazy and
              adds fetchpriority=high instead

    width and height are always emitted. They are what stop the page shifting
    under someone's thumb while the photos arrive. -#}
{% macro picture(name, alt, sizes, eager=false, klass="") %}
{%- set p = photos[name] -%}
{%- if not p or not p.widths.length -%}
{#- A photo uploaded through the admin panel. It has no -400/-640 variants,
    because those are made by a script that runs on a laptop and Ava does not
    have one — she has a phone, and what comes off a phone is four megabytes.

    So these go through Netlify's Image CDN, which resizes on request. Same
    width ladder as everything else, so a phone still pulls roughly 400px of
    photo rather than the whole thing. It costs nothing extra: transformed
    images are billed as ordinary bandwidth and requests, and they are smaller
    than the file they replace.

    One <img>, no <picture>. Asked for a width and nothing else, Netlify picks
    the format itself from what the browser says it accepts, which means AVIF
    where AVIF is understood and JPEG where it is not. Pinning fm=webp in a
    <source> would have thrown away the better of those two.

    The curated photos deliberately do not use this. They already have real
    variants that work in any local preview, and routing them through a URL that
    only resolves once deployed would mean the site could no longer be checked
    properly before it went out. -#}
{%- set src = ("/assets/" + (p.original if p else name)) | imgSrc -%}
<img{{ ' class="' + klass + '"' | safe if klass }} src="/.netlify/images?url={{ src }}&amp;w=640" sizes="{{ sizes }}" srcset="{% for w in [400, 640, 900] %}/.netlify/images?url={{ src }}&amp;w={{ w }} {{ w }}w{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}" alt="{{ alt }}"{% if eager %} fetchpriority="high"{% else %} loading="lazy"{% endif %}{% if p and p.width %} width="{{ p.width }}" height="{{ p.height }}"{% endif %}>
{%- else -%}
<picture>
  <source type="image/webp" sizes="{{ sizes }}" srcset="{% for w in p.webp %}assets/{{ name }}-{{ w }}.webp {{ w }}w{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}">
  <img{{ ' class="' + klass + '"' | safe if klass }} src="assets/{{ name }}-{{ p.fallback }}.jpg" sizes="{{ sizes }}" srcset="{% for w in p.widths %}assets/{{ name }}-{{ w }}.jpg {{ w }}w{{ ", " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}" alt="{{ alt }}"{% if eager %} fetchpriority="high"{% else %} loading="lazy"{% endif %} width="{{ p.width }}" height="{{ p.height }}">
</picture>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}
